vps-ready <version> on Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie) (docker container on vm-other)
Audit: 5 passed, 5 failed (1 high, 3 medium, 1 low). vps-ready can fix all of them.

? What will this server run?
  > Something else: only SSH is open (recommended: no Docker or web server found)
    Web apps or Docker: opens ports 80 and 443
? Admin user for logging in: admin
  new account: logs in with 1 key copied from /root/.ssh/authorized_keys; sudo
  without a password.
  Root and password logins will be turned off, so this key is how you log in.
All other incoming ports are blocked.
? More ports to open, e.g. 8080/tcp or 53/udp (Enter for none): 
? Pick which modules to run yourself? (y/N) yes
? Modules to run (Space toggles, Enter continues)
  [ ] user.admin: Set up an admin account that logs in with an SSH key and uses sudo (can lock you out; undone unless you confirm)
  [ ] ssh.server: Turn off root and password logins over SSH (can lock you out; undone unless you confirm)
  [ ] deploy.docker: Install Docker with logs capped and ports on loopback by default
  [ ] firewall.inbound: Drop incoming traffic that no rule allows (can lock you out; undone unless you confirm)
  [ ] ssh.bruteforce: Ban addresses that keep failing to log in over SSH
  [x] kernel.sysctl: Harden kernel settings (does not apply: kernel settings belong to the host inside a container)
  [ ] updates.auto: Install security updates automatically
  [ ] deploy.proxy: Serve sites over HTTPS with Caddy, proxied to their upstreams
  [ ] deploy.user: Set up a deploy account whose SSH keys are restricted

kernel.sysctl: not applicable (kernel settings belong to the host inside a container)

Nothing to do with the modules you chose.

? Save your answers to vps-ready.toml to repeat them later? (Y/n) no
Not saved. To repeat this, save these lines to a file and run:
  sudo vps-ready apply --profile <file>

schema = 1
extends = "minimal"

[modules."firewall.inbound"]
enabled = false

[modules."ssh.server"]
enabled = false

[modules."updates.auto"]
enabled = false

[modules."user.admin"]
enabled = false
